(window.LADDER_CARDS["29a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["29a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"29a1.1","q":"What does the outside-angle technique say, and why does it hold in every geometry that obeys the other axioms?","a":"an outside angle of a triangle is larger than each of the two inside angles away from it; no line of its proof asks whether two lines meet, so it never touches the parallel question","w":["an outside angle equals the sum of the two inside angles away from it; that needs the parallel axiom","an outside angle is smaller than each inside angle away from it; it holds on the sphere too","an outside angle is larger than the inside angle beside it; it holds because angles add to π"],"why":"It uses a midpoint and matching triangles only."},
 {"u":"29a2.1","q":"What does the never-meet technique say, which half of the parallel question do the other axioms settle, and which half remains?","a":"two lines at right angles to the same line never meet; so through a point off a line there is at least one line that never meets it, which the other axioms give; the parallel axiom asks for at most one, and that half is the whole question","w":["two lines at right angles to one line meet far away; the other axioms give at most one parallel","two lines at right angles to one line never meet; the other axioms give exactly one parallel, so nothing remains","lines that never meet do not exist without the parallel axiom; both halves remain"],"why":"If they met, the triangle would break the outside-angle technique."},
 {"u":"29a3.1","q":"What is the upright figure, what does the top-angles technique say, and what does it leave open?","a":"a four-sided figure with two equal sides standing at right angles on a base; its two top angles are equal; it leaves open whether they are right, both blunt or both sharp","w":["a square; its top angles are right; nothing is left open","a triangle standing on its base; its top angle is sharp; the base angles are left open","a four-sided figure with four equal sides; its top angles add to π; their equality is left open"],"why":"Equal, then. But equal to what?"},
 {"u":"29a4.1","q":"What does the no-more-than-straight technique say, what does the halving move make, and how does the refutation use it?","a":"the angles of every triangle add to at most π; the halving move makes a new triangle with the same angle sum and one angle at most half of a chosen one; repeated, if the sum were above π an angle becomes so small that the other two add to more than π, which the outside-angle technique forbids","w":["the angles add to exactly π; the halving move cuts the triangle into two with half the sum each","the angles add to at least π; the halving move doubles an angle until it passes π","the angles add to at most π; the halving move halves every side, and the refutation measures the small triangle"],"why":"Two angles of a triangle always add to less than π."},
 {"u":"29a5.1","q":"What does the no-blunt-top technique say, and why was the trial of 1733 not over at its last line?","a":"the top angles of an upright figure are right or sharp, never blunt; the sharp case was declared repugnant to the nature of the straight line, but that last line cited no axiom, and strange is not a false line","w":["the top angles are always right; the trial ended with a proof of the parallel axiom","the top angles are never sharp; the trial was not over because the blunt case remained","the top angles are blunt or right; the trial failed through an error in its first line"],"why":"29c finishes it."},
 {"u":"29a6.1","q":"What is wrong with “S ≤ π for every triangle, so S = π”?","a":"at most is not exactly: the other axioms rule out more than π and leave less than π open, which is the sharp case","w":["S ≤ π was never proved","S = π follows once one triangle is measured","S ≥ π is what the other axioms give"],"why":"The disc plane has every S below π."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["29b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["29b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"29b1.1","q":"What are the lines of the sphere, what does the lines-always-meet technique say, and which of the other axioms does the sphere break?","a":"the great circles, cut by planes through the centre; two different great circles meet in exactly two opposite points; so two opposite points lie on endlessly many lines, which breaks the first axiom, one line through two points","w":["every circle on the sphere; two of them may never meet; the sphere breaks only the parallel axiom","the great circles; two of them meet in exactly one point; the sphere breaks no other axiom","the meridians only; they meet at the poles; the sphere breaks the axiom of right angles"],"why":"A different geometry, not a counterexample inside the old one."},
 {"u":"29b2.1","q":"What is a lune, what does the lune-area technique say, and how is the area reached from n lunes that fill the sphere?","a":"the piece of a sphere between two great half circles that meet at opposite points at an angle α; its area is 2αR²; n equal lunes of angle 2π/n fill the sphere, so each has 4πR²/n, m side by side have m of those, and such fractions squeeze every α from both sides","w":["a triangle with two right angles; its area is αR²; reached by cutting it into n triangles","the piece between two great half circles; its area is 4αR²; reached by totalling in ball measures only","half of the sphere; its area is 2πR²; no squeeze is needed"],"why":"The whole sphere is the lune of angle 2π."},
 {"u":"29b3.1","q":"What does the excess-area technique say, and why does every triangle on a sphere have angles adding to more than π?","a":"a triangle on a sphere of radius R has area (α + β + γ − π)R²; an area is more than 0, so the angles add to more than π","w":["its area is (α + β + γ)R²; the angles add to more than π because the sides are curved","its area is (π − α − β − γ)R²; the angles add to less than π","its area is half the product of two sides; the angle sum is π as on the flat"],"why":"The blunt case of 29a lives here, since the sphere breaks another axiom."},
 {"u":"29b4.1","q":"What does the same-angles-same-size technique say, and why do a sphere’s angles carry a length scale?","a":"on a sphere two triangles with the same three angles have the same area, and a small triangle has a small excess; the excess is the area over R², so measuring the angles of a large enough triangle gives R","w":["on a sphere triangles with the same angles may have any size, as on the flat; angles carry no scale","two triangles with the same angles have the same area only when R = 1; the scale comes from the sides","a small triangle has a large excess; the angles give the area but never R"],"why":"There are no scale copies on a sphere."},
 {"u":"29b5.1","q":"What is wrong with “the circles of latitude 40° and 50° are parallel lines on the sphere”?","a":"they never meet, but they are not lines of the sphere: only great circles are, and of the circles of latitude only the equator is one","w":["they do meet, at the poles","they are lines, but not parallel, since they have different lengths","nothing; the sphere has many parallels"],"why":"Every two lines of the sphere meet."});
